---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MemeGenerator from '@/components/tools/image-edit/MemeGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="add-text-to-image">
  <MemeGenerator client:load variant="text" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Putting words on a picture without a design app</h2>
    <p>
      Usually you need one line of text, in a readable colour, in the correct corner, exported at the
      original resolution. This page adds text to an image that way. It opens with one caption box, a
      normal sans-serif face, no outline and a soft drop shadow. On a photograph, these settings look
      deliberate and not loud.
    </p>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in the photo, screenshot or graphic.</li>
      <li>Type your text into the box on the right. Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> in it for a line break. Long lines wrap at the wrap width you set.</li>
      <li>Drag the text on the canvas to place it. Keyboard users can select the box and use the arrow keys.</li>
      <li>Set the colour, and add an outline or a drop shadow so the text stays readable on any background.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Add text box</strong> for each new label. For example, a title and a credit line can have different settings.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Making text readable over any background</h2>
    <p>
      The most common mistake is white text on a photo that is white in one corner. Three methods fix
      it, and you can combine them:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>An outline: a dark stroke at 5–10% of the font size traces each letter. It is the most reliable option over busy detail such as leaves or a crowd.</li>
      <li>A drop shadow is softer. It lifts light text off a mid-tone background without the comic-book look of an outline.</li>
      <li>A bar: use the white-space sliders to add a solid band above or below the photo and put the text there. It gives the best legibility and keeps the image clear.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Choosing a size that survives the feed</h2>
    <p>
      Size is a percentage of image height, not pixels, so the same setting looks the same on an 800 px
      or a 6000 px source. As a guide: 3–4% for a discreet credit or watermark, 6–8% for a caption read
      at a glance, and 10% or more for a headline that must be legible as a thumbnail.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Use</th><th>Size</th><th>Style</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Photo credit / handle</td><td>2.5–4%</td><td>Small sans, a mid grey for a 60–80% opacity look, bottom-right</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Social caption</td><td>6–8%</td><td>Drop shadow on, sentence case, wide wrap width</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Poster headline</td><td>10–16%</td><td>Heavy sans, uppercase, thin outline</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Diagram label</td><td>3–5%</td><td>Monospace, solid contrasting colour, no shadow</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Fonts come from your device, never from a CDN</h2>
    <p>
      The font list has the families that come with Windows, macOS, iOS and Android. The page does not
      fetch Google Fonts or other remote typefaces, because it makes no network requests. If your
      device does not have a font, the browser uses the next match in the same stack. Thus the same
      settings can look slightly different on a Mac and on Android. The exported image does not
      change: when the text is drawn into the pixels, it looks the same everywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>Watermarking, honestly</h2>
    <p>
      A drawn watermark deters reuse but does not protect the photo. It makes reuse without credit
      harder, and a screenshot still shows your name. Anyone can still crop, clone or inpaint it. For
      proof of origin, keep an untouched original with its EXIF data in a safe place, and publish the
      marked copy. To remove identifying data before you publish, use
      <a href="/remove-exif-data">Remove EXIF Data</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>After you export</h2>
    <p>
      PNG keeps text edges sharp and is the correct choice for screenshots and flat graphics. JPG is
      smaller for photographs. If the result must fit a size limit,
      <a href="/compress-image-to-200kb">compress it to 200 KB</a>. A text overlay compresses well, so
      the caption stays sharp at a small target. For a caption under the image, the
      <a href="/meme-generator">meme generator</a> has a white-bar preset.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
